Holiday sales are looking good, according to the most recent prediction from the National Retail Federation . They believe that retail sales in November and December will grow 4.2% over 2025.
That translates to total spending of $1.013 trillion. By comparison, last year&rsquo;s holiday sales rose 3.1% over 2024, totaling $972 billion.
&ldquo;This year&rsquo;s forecast reflects our confidence that consumers will prioritize the holiday season even as they remain mindful of their budgets,&rdquo; NRF CEO Matthew Shay said in a statement. &ldquo;Retailers are prepared to meet that demand with competitive prices, value and choices for every budget.&rdquo;
&ldquo;We head into the holiday season with strong spending momentum, as consumers remain the bright spot in the economy,&rdquo; NRF Chief Economist Mark Mathews said. &ldquo;At the same time, inflation, rising gas prices and pressure on household spending will affect consumers differently across income groups.&rdquo;
Retailers are hiring seasonal workers to meet consumer demand this holiday season. NRF expects retailers to hire between 325,000 and 375,000 seasonal workers, in line with last year&rsquo;s estimated 356,000 seasonal jobs.
Similar to last year, retailers remain cautious amid higher gas prices, persistent inflation, higher interest rates and elevated consumer debt, resulting in a measured approach to seasonal hiring.
